WEBVTT

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:06.550
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department CSULB.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:12.630
Now that we have introduced the example of Moore FSM, that we are going to follow, let

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:18.170
us look at each of the segments in three segment code.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:22.190
So let us first start with a state register.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:29.230
For state register, we are going to use same code as we used in previous lab for basic

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.230
register.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:41.190
As you see below here, in Oliver's statement in sensitivity list, we have two different

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.030
signals, two different variables.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:50.990
We have clock and reset, and in case reset is active, the state of the state register

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:57.310
is going to become all zeros as per this line 25.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:02.310
if reset is zero, this basic register is going to update

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.830
on every positive edge of clock cycle.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.990
The code for basic register is always going to be the same

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.030
because in the assignment,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:21.030
we are always going to say state gets the value of n state,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.750
basically next state, and the value of next state

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.630
is going to be defined in a separate segment

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.250
that describes next date logic.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.250
Also, please note that because we are describing

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.190
the state register, which is a synchronous

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:47.350
sequential element, we are going to use

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:52.350
this kind of assignment inside of the always block.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:02:02.350
And that is what is characteristic for sequential circuits.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:10.350
Please refer to one of the first slides where we define blocking and non-blocking assignments

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:17.980
and refresh your memory regarding those two.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.700
Next coding segment is coding segment,

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.100
which is computational circuit

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.120
that is implementing next state logic.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.660
All this block is going to be

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.740
a little bit different this time

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.500
because in sensitivity list,

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.500
we do not have clock and reset as in previous example.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.500
If we want to make this explicit,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.300
we will put here in sensitivity list

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:51.300
all the inputs that our combinational circuit has.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:56.380
In this case, that would be state and enable signals.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.940
However, to be on the safe side,

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.800
and this is much easier to implement,

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.540
we can also put a wildcard in parenthesis,

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.160
and that is going to replace all inputs,

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.100
and it's also going to save us

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:20.100
for potentially making a mistake of specifying

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.810
incomplete sensitivity list.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:29.730
Another important thing for coding sequential circuit

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:34.730
is that all the outputs always need to be

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.830
assigned value too.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.410
So that is while in line 32,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.490
and you might have noticed that when we were coding

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:49.910
for encoder and decoder as well,

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:54.910
we assign a value of n state with some value

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.090
and here is going to be a value of current state.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.370
The reason is as follows.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:07.370
If by any chance we would have a very complex expression

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:12.250
for case S zero and n state,

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.030
and we would have more than one state,

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.390
So let's say more than one output.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:21.450
Let's say we have net end state, we have A and B.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:25.630
And for S zero, we assign N state and A,

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.810
but forget to assign B.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.810
For B, the tool will instantiate a memory element

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:36.630
or a latch.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:41.630
To avoid that, it is prudent to after begin

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:53.350
in, and before we start case statement initialize all the values of all outputs of our combinational

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.010
circuit.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:05:00.930
The other thing that we need to notice, so we have case statement that depends on the

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:12.330
current state, here calls state, and when state is equal to S0, and state becomes equal to

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:17.610
the value of this expression. We already are a little bit acquainted with this. So this

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:29.510
is a ternary operand where the result is going to be equal to this expression if the

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:37.110
value in parentheses is true or to this expression if value in parentheses is

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:45.230
false. In this case in general for this operand in parentheses we can have

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:52.870
either Boolean variable or Boolean expression that we evaluate. So the next

00:05:52.870 --> 00:06:03.910
state becomes S1 if enable is 1 or it stays S0 if enable is a 0 and

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:11.710
we move to this state carry statement as usual evaluating if the state is equal

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:19.670
to S0 or S1 or if state is by some accident or mistake none of these then

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:24.770
and we assign default case.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:28.810
The last portion of this code is, again,

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:32.370
combinational circuit that describes output logic.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:36.210
The code is very similar to the one

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.090
that we saw on the previous slide.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:42.930
Same applies to the sensitivity list

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.330
here in always statement,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.850
and to the assigning values,

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:53.810
all the outputs of combinational circuit.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:58.810
The difference here is that we do not have any conditions.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.610
Because this is more FSM,

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:07.410
each output corresponds to a state.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.690
So basically for S0 output is always going to be

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.250
as shown here on the right-hand side.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:19.210
and we can easily copy the values of the output

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:23.190
as given in the state transition diagram over here.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:29.890
What I usually like to do is I like to put unused states

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:34.210
here for initial value and for default value

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.370
just so that in simulation should something go wrong,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:43.370
I have an indicator that basically none of these states

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:54.250
were evaluated as correct and we have indicator that things did not go as planned.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:58.250
Thank you for watching. Hope this was helpful.

